rows.reply:
Under federal law, a child of a member of a statutory health insurer is covered through Familienversicherung (family cover for dependants under statutory insurance). SGB V §10(1) states: "Versichert sind ... die Kinder von Mitgliedern".

The law attaches five conditions to every family member covered this way. The family member must:
1. have their residence or habitual abode in Germany ("ihren Wohnsitz oder gewöhnlichen Aufenthalt im Inland");
2. not be compulsorily insured under §5(1) nos. 1 to 8, 11 to 12, and not be voluntarily insured;
3. not be exempt from insurance or exempted from compulsory insurance;
4. not be mainly self-employed;
5. not have a total income that regularly exceeds one seventh of the monthly reference value under §18 SGB IV.

Under §10(2), children are covered up to age 18, up to 23 if not employed, and up to 25 if in school or vocational training or doing a voluntary social or ecological year. There is no age limit for children with disabilities who cannot support themselves, if the disability existed while they were family-insured within those age limits.

There is one exclusion for children in §10(3). Children are not covered if the parent related to them who is the member's spouse or civil partner meets all of these conditions: they are not a member of a Krankenkasse, their total income regularly exceeds one twelfth of the annual earnings ceiling ("ein Zwölftel der Jahresarbeitsentgeltgrenze"), and that income is regularly higher than the member's. The excerpts do not say how the child is insured in that case.

If the conditions are met more than once, for example if both parents are members, the member chooses the Krankenkasse (§10(5)).

The step you take: the member must report the family-insured persons to the responsible Krankenkasse, with the information needed to run the Familienversicherung, and must also report later changes to that information (§10(6)). The national association of statutory health insurers sets a uniform procedure and uniform reporting forms for this.

The birth certificate is needed to register with the Krankenkasse. You must register the child within 1 week at the Standesamt responsible for the place of birth, and the Standesamt issues the birth certificate. If the child was born in a hospital, birth centre or maternity clinic, the facility notifies the Standesamt and sends the birth confirmation.

Whether your child meets these conditions is decided by your Krankenkasse, not here.

I can't fill in your Elterngeld (parental allowance) application. The form needs choices and figures specific to your family, such as which months to claim and your income, and only the responsible authority decides your case. Here is what the law and the official guidance say the application involves.

How it is made. Elterngeld must be applied for in writing ("Elterngeld ist schriftlich zu beantragen", BEEG §7(1)). Payment is backdated only for the last three months of the child's life before the month in which the application is received. Earlier months are not paid.

What the application must state. For each month of the child's life you claim, the application must say whether it is for basic Elterngeld, Elterngeld Plus or the partnership bonus (BEEG §7(1)). These choices can be changed until the end of the payment period. A change can be backdated only three months, and for months already paid it is not allowed except in cases of particular hardship. One exception: for a month in which Elterngeld Plus was already received, basic Elterngeld can be claimed afterwards (BEEG §7(2)).

Who signs. The person applying signs. The other entitled parent also signs to confirm they have seen it. There are two exceptions: the case under §4c, and an application by a person with sole custody (BEEG §7(3)). At the same time, the other parent can either 1. apply for Elterngeld themselves, or 2. tell the authority how many monthly amounts they claim. If the authority has neither, all monthly amounts are paid to the applicant, and the other parent can later receive only what remains of the joint entitlement.

Income stated in advance. If the application gives expected earnings during the payment period, actual earnings must be proven after that period ends (BEEG §8(1)).

Documents to attach, per the Familienportal:
- your child's birth certificate;
- proof of previous income. Employed mothers: pay slips for the 12 months before the month maternity protection begins. Employed fathers: pay slips for the 12 months before the birth. Self-employed: usually your last tax assessment;
- employed mothers: certificates from the health insurer about maternity benefit after the birth, and from the employer about supplements to it;
- civil servants or soldiers (mothers): certificates of pay during maternity protection and of supplements to it;
- mothers with private health insurance and daily sickness allowance cover: the insurer's certificates of that allowance during maternity protection;
- if you will earn while receiving Elterngeld: an employer certificate of your working hours during that time, or, if self-employed, your own statement of previous and planned working hours. If you will work less than before, statements about arrangements made to relieve you may also be needed, for example hiring a substitute, colleagues taking over tasks, or fewer contracts.
Further documents may be needed depending on the case. Your employer or Dienstherr must issue the documents you need.

Who decides. Each state designates the competent authority. The responsible one is the authority for the district where your child lives when you first apply (BEEG §12(1)). In practice this is your local Elterngeld office, which assesses your application. It is also responsible for advice on parental leave (BEEG §12(2)).

Whether your application will be approved is something I cannot assess. Only the responsible authority decides an individual case: the Elterngeld office for your district. Here are the general conditions the law sets.

Who is entitled. Under BEEG §1(1), Elterngeld (parental allowance) goes to a person who meets all four conditions:
1. has a residence or habitual abode in Germany ("einen Wohnsitz oder seinen gewöhnlichen Aufenthalt in Deutschland");
2. lives in one household with the child;
3. cares for and brings up the child personally;
4. is not working, or is not working full-time ("keine oder keine volle Erwerbstätigkeit").
For multiple births there is only one entitlement.

What counts as not working full-time. Under §1(6), a person is not fully employed if:
1. their working time does not exceed 32 hours a week on average over the child's month of life;
2. they are in vocational training; or
3. they work as a suitable childminder under §§23 and 43 SGB VIII.

Non-EU nationals. Under §1(7), a foreign national without EU freedom of movement is entitled only if they hold:
1. a Niederlassungserlaubnis or an Erlaubnis zum Daueraufenthalt-EU;
2. an EU Blue Card, ICT card, Mobile ICT card, or a residence permit that allows, or allowed, employment for at least six months. Exceptions: permits issued for training, au-pair work, seasonal work, European volunteer service, or job-seeking under §20a(5) sentence 1 (letter a). Also excluded are permits for study, recognition of foreign qualifications, or job-seeking (letter b), unless the person is working, on parental leave under §15, or receiving benefits under SGB III. Permits issued because of war in the home country under §23(1), or under §23a or §25(3) to (5) of the Residence Act, are excluded too (letter c);
3. a permit named in letter c, while lawfully working, on parental leave under §15, or receiving SGB III benefits;
4. a permit named in letter c, and having stayed in Germany lawfully, with permission or tolerated, for at least 15 months; or
5. a Beschäftigungsduldung under §60d with §60a(2) sentence 3 of the Residence Act.
A minor is entitled under number 3 regardless of employment.

Income limit. Under §1(8), the entitlement ends if the person had taxable income of more than 175,000 euros in the last completed assessment period before the birth. If another person also meets the conditions (for example, the other parent in the same household), the limit applies to the two incomes added together.

Applying. Under §7(1), Elterngeld must be applied for in writing ("ist schriftlich zu beantragen"). It is paid retroactively only for the last three months of the child's life before the month in which the application is received. Earlier months are not paid. The application must state which months are claimed as Basiselterngeld, Elterngeld Plus or Partnerschaftsbonus. Under §7(3), the other entitled parent also signs to confirm they have seen it, except under §4c or where one parent has sole custody.

Provisional decisions. Under §8(3), the amount is decided provisionally if any of three situations applies:
1. the tax assessment for the last year before the birth is not yet available and it cannot yet be said whether the §1(8) limit is exceeded;
2. income before the birth cannot be determined; or
3. the applicant expects to have earnings while receiving Elterngeld.

If you disagree with a decision, the Familienportal states that an objection must be lodged in writing with your Elterngeld office within one month of receiving the decision ("innerhalb eines Monats nach Zugang").
